-- title:
--   Whittaker coefficients as sums of translated Jacquet–Whittaker functions
-- statement:
--   Let $v$ be a height-one prime of $\mathbb{Z}=\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$, with completion $\mathbb{Q}_v$, and let $\chi_0,\chi_1,\chi_2$ be multiplicative homomorphisms $\mathbb{Q}_v^{\times}\to\mathbb{C}^{\times}$, each assumed locally constant. Write `principalSeries3 v χ` for the $\mathbb{C}$-subspace of functions $f$ on $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q}_v)$ that are locally constant, invariant under left multiplication by the upper unipotent matrices `upperUnipotent3 x y z`, and satisfy $f(\mathrm{diag}(a_0,a_1,a_2)g)=\big(\prod_i\chi_i(a_i)\big)\cdot(\lVert a_0\rVert/\lVert a_2\rVert)\cdot f(g)$. Let $\Lambda$ be a $\mathbb{C}$-linear form on this space which is a Whittaker functional for the inverse of the standard local additive character, i.e. $\Lambda\big(F(\,\cdot\;\mathtt{upperUnipotent3}\,x\,y\,z)\big)=\psi_v^{-1}(x+y)\,\Lambda(F)$ for all $x,y,z\in\mathbb{Q}_v$ and all $F$, where $\psi_v=$ `psiLocal ℚ v`; and let $f$ be a vector of the principal series. The assertion is that there exist $n\in\mathbb{N}$, scalars $\kappa_j\in\mathbb{C}$, elements $x_j,y_j,z_j\in\mathbb{Q}_v$ and functions $\Phi_j$ on $\mathbb{Q}_v^3$, each locally constant with compact support, such that the matrix coefficient $h\mapsto\Lambda\big(f(\,\cdot\,h)\big)$ equals $h\mapsto\sum_j\kappa_j\,\mathtt{jacquetWhittaker3}\,v\,\chi\,\Phi_j\big(\mathrm{diag}(1,-1,1)\,h\,\mathtt{upperUnipotent3}(x_j,y_j,z_j)\,w_0\big)$, where `jacquetWhittaker3 v χ Φ` sends $g$ to the `jacquetValue` of the right translate by $g$ of the big-cell section `cellSectionOf v χ Φ`, and $w_0$ is the antidiagonal permutation matrix $\mathtt{antidiagonal3}$.
--
--   This is the $\psi_v^{-1}$-variant of the explicit description of the Whittaker model of a principal series of $\mathrm{GL}_3$ over a $p$-adic field: every Whittaker coefficient function is a finite combination of Jacquet–Whittaker integrals of cell sections, translated on the left by $\mathrm{diag}(1,-1,1)$ (which converts $\psi_v$ into $\psi_v^{-1}$) and on the right by unipotent elements times the long Weyl element. It supplies the explicit test vectors used in the local Rankin–Selberg computations for $\mathrm{GL}_3\times\mathrm{GL}_2$ and in the analysis of the cyclic subspace generated by such coefficient functions.
